19.2  ELF and "command not found"

TL;DR
  - "command not found": no file of that name in any folder of your PATH.
    Install the package, or call the program by its full path.
  - "No such file or directory" for a file that exists: what's missing is its
    interpreter, the #! line of a script, or the loader a binary was built for.
  - "Exec format error": the binary is built for another CPU (ARM vs x86).

      file ./mytool          # architecture, linking, interpreter
      head -1 deploy.sh      # the #! line of a script

On your machine
  PATH      /home/alice/.local/bin
            /usr/local/bin (doesn't exist)
            /usr/bin
            . ⚠ the current folder, wherever you are
  Repeated  /usr/bin (listed more than once: harmless, the first one wins)
  Libraries LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/oracle/lib
  Runs      programs built for x86-64, glibc

Pitfalls
  - A script saved with Windows line endings asks for /bin/bash\r: "bad
    interpreter". Fix it with dos2unix or sed -i 's/\r$//' deploy.sh.
  - "Permission denied" on your own file: it lacks the x bit (chmod +x), or sits
    on a filesystem mounted noexec.
  - Programs built for musl (Alpine) don't run on glibc systems, nor the other
    way round, unless they are statically linked.
  - To find the package that provides a command: apt-file search bin/NAME
    (Debian, Ubuntu), dnf provides '*/bin/NAME' (Fedora), pacman -F NAME (Arch).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 19.2 --why

